WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.470
For decades, Hollywood has derived blockbuster
drama from natural disasters both real and

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:06.470
imagined.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:10.590
From the all-star disaster movie classics
of the '70s through the effects-driven spectacles

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.179
of more recent decades, there's nothing quite
like the thrill of watching things go horribly

00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:18.810
awry from the comfort of your couch or local
cineplex.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:23.250
In the interest of saving you some time — since,
as these films remind us, the clock is indeed

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:28.119
ticking for us all — here's a look at some
disaster films that simply can't be missed.

00:00:28.119 --> 00:00:29.230
The Poseidon Adventure

00:00:29.230 --> 00:00:36.449
2006's Poseidon was a regrettable remake of
1972's The Poseidon Adventure and should be

00:00:36.449 --> 00:00:37.989
ignored at all costs.

00:00:37.989 --> 00:00:42.149
However, the original, about a group of luxury
liner passengers trapped in an upside-down

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:46.129
vessel that's slowly but surely sinking, is
well worth wading through.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:50.569
Anchored by Gene Hackman and Shelley Winters,
the film presents a gripping look at what

00:00:50.569 --> 00:00:55.379
might happen to a group of well-off vacationers
when a tidal wave capsizes their ship and

00:00:55.379 --> 00:00:58.800
leaves them scrambling for escape from a watery
grave.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.989
Special effects have obviously evolved since
then, but the patient pace and fully realized

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:07.289
characters are increasingly rare among the
modern crop of blockbuster action movies.

00:01:07.289 --> 00:01:13.360
"I started to believe in your promises, that
we had a chance."

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.460
The Towering Inferno

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:21.700
Starring Paul Newman, Steve McQueen, Faye
Dunaway, and Fred Astaire, The Towering Inferno

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.981
was star-studded to say the least.

00:01:23.981 --> 00:01:27.979
The film centers on the blazing drama that
spreads after the world's tallest building

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:32.469
— the Glass Tower — goes up in flames
after an electrical circuit goes haywire during

00:01:32.469 --> 00:01:34.720
the Tower's dedication ceremony.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.200
The blaze leaves entire families trapped dozens
of stories above the ground.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.899
And beneath the obvious appeal of The Towering
Inferno's survival story is a cutting commentary

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:47.869
on corporate greed and the true cost of the
bottom line.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:49.530
Alive

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:53.510
Inspired by a real-life Uruguayan rugby team
who were stranded in the snow-capped Andes

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.969
mountains after their small plane crashed,
Alive is a chillingly raw depiction of the

00:01:57.969 --> 00:02:02.689
group's efforts to survive the accident — and
the cruel climate where they were stranded.

00:02:02.689 --> 00:02:07.149
Based on Piers Paul Read's written account,
the movie spares no bitter details about what

00:02:07.149 --> 00:02:11.420
these athletes had to do to crawl through
each day until rescue arrived.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.739
From the drawn-out suffering of gangrene and
frostbite to their desperate decision to nourish

00:02:15.739 --> 00:02:19.959
themselves on the flesh of the fallen, the
fact that it's based on a true story makes

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:23.500
the cinematic experience that much more unsettling.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.500
Outbreak

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.940
At a time when the spread of communicable
diseases was a growing social concern, thanks

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:34.290
in large part to the uncertainty surrounding
AIDS, Wolfgang Petersen's Outbreak capitalized

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.879
on those fears spectacularly.

00:02:35.879 --> 00:02:38.290
"Dammit, Sam I wanna save these people same
as you."

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.760
Thanks to its deadly virus going airborne
and remaining undetectable until symptoms

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:48.750
started presenting in infected patients, Outbreak
made everyone a potential threat — and showcased

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:53.901
the societal collapse and militaristic governmental
oppression that could so easily unfold in

00:02:53.901 --> 00:02:55.200
the wake of such a threat.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:59.849
It wasn't the first or last film to explore
such a viral phenomenon, but the climate of

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:03.891
the film's release, combined with the breadth
of its fictional disease's impact, made it

00:03:03.891 --> 00:03:05.610
a must-see.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:06.739
Daylight

00:03:06.739 --> 00:03:11.409
The brawny action prowess of Sylvester Stallone
in his prime in Rob Cohen's Daylight offered

00:03:11.409 --> 00:03:15.209
a spectacular foundation for Sly the Savior
at his best.

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:19.700
Stallone starred as a former EMS medic who
puts himself in harm's way to help those who

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:24.150
became trapped in New York's Holland Tunnel
after a group of diamond thieves crash into

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.810
a chemical waste truck, triggering an explosion.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:31.720
It all adds up to an action-packed race against
time — and the Hudson River — set in an

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:33.650
eerie darkness.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.650
Twister

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.760
This pic might be best remembered for its
giant storms and special effects, but Twister's

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:43.150
superior acting and emotional subplots are
what made the movie such a standout.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.480
Sure, the dialogue can get a little silly
…

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.700
"Cow!"

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.000
"I gotta go, Julia, we got cows!"

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:59.720
But for the most part, Twister does a terrific
job of balancing human drama — like a dissolving

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.049
marriage and a character's childhood trauma
— against the effects-driven chaos audiences

00:04:04.049 --> 00:04:06.650
demand from a film about tornado chasers.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.739
It's still very easy to be swept up in, even
decades later.

00:04:10.739 --> 00:04:12.760
Dante's Peak

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:18.130
Audiences had their pick of magma-movies in
1997, with Dante's Peak and Volcano both erupting

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.100
in quick succession at theaters.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.780
But whereas Volcano threatened the entire
metropolitan area of Los Angeles, Dante's

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.790
Peak benefited from a narrower focus, zooming
in on a small town and letting viewers get

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.400
to know and care about its characters.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:37.570
Additionally, Peak's hero relied on his scientific
expertise, rather than sheer bravado.

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:41.690
And while Volcano leans heavily on ordinary
action movie conventions to carry the plot

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:46.290
along, Dante's Peak offers some upsettingly
creative scenes of chaos that still hold up

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:47.890
even now.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.160
Deep Impact

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.520
Mimi Leder's Deep Impact was miles above Michael
Bay's Armageddon.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:57.520
Rather than relying on a ridiculous action
thriller fantasy of sending cowboy oil drillers

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:03.150
into space — y'know, instead of actual astronauts
— Deep Impact actually relied on the expertise

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.120
of scientists, and it shows.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.600
More importantly, the movie wasn't just about
showcasing the heroism of a few astronauts,

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.010
but focused on the scattered impact and personal
sacrifices that could result from impending

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.070
doom in various parts of the world.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:20.030
There are still some efforts to thwart the
rock from colliding with Earth, of course,

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.880
but the most memorable moments are grounded
in the smaller-scale drama that holds the

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.960
sci-fi action together.

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.150
The Wave

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:32.620
Plenty of disaster movies have used avalanches
or tsunamis to fuel the action, but Roar Uthaug's

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:37.630
The Wave stands out due to its stunning cinematography
and its main characters' intensely affecting

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:38.630
arc.

00:05:38.630 --> 00:05:43.440
Nothing about the movie is particularly groundbreaking,
but its obedience to formula is easy to forgive

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:48.440
thanks to Uthaug's visual style and a screenplay
that's driven as much by its relatable characters

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.260
as it is by the titular catastrophe.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.560
The Impossible

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.750
The Indian Ocean tsunami of 2004 cost hundreds
of thousands of human lives and leveled coastlines

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.440
in over a dozen countries.

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.580
Any movie that dared to dramatize this real-life
disaster would have to deal with the story

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:08.780
sensitively — and J.A. Bayona's The Impossible
did just that.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.030
The film waded through the endless misery
of the story to find a glimmering, albeit

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:17.940
somewhat controversial, piece of hope in the
Belon family, who defied the odds and managed

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:19.490
to survive the storm.

00:06:19.490 --> 00:06:23.721
As with all the best disaster movies, the
viewing experience can be grueling, but it

00:06:23.721 --> 00:06:25.590
pays inspiring dividends.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.760
And as a bonus?

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:27.760
Check out the young Spider-Man!

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:28.950
*screaming*

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:32.880
Thanks for watching!

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.870
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:36.870 --> 00:06:39.260
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

